    <shape type="text" topLeftX="616" topLeftY="374" width="276" height="84">
      <content textType="body" fontSize="13" fontFamily="Inter" color="rgba(15,36,64,1)" lineSpacing="multiple:1.35" textAlign="left">
        <p>Why it matters: the bank is the boundary we cannot change - so its quirks are quarantined, not spread.</p>
      </content>
    </shape>
    <shape type="text" topLeftX="856" topLeftY="510" width="48" height="18">
      <content textType="caption" fontSize="12" fontFamily="Inter" color="rgba(138,148,166,1)" textAlign="right" wrap="false">
        <p>12 / 24</p>
      </content>
    </shape>
  </data>
  <note>
    <content textType="body">
      <p>Stop six is where we build the message the bank actually wants. I use a separate DTO for the bank request, and the conversion happens in one place - this is the Anti-Corruption Layer pattern. The bank wants the month zero-padded and the cvv as a string; those quirks are absorbed here and never leak into the rest of the code. And because BankPaymentRequest overrides toString to mask the card number, even a careless debug log cannot print a full PAN.</p>
    </content>
  </note>
